A1. An instrument with an old twist. ~ CORKSCREW
A9. Dry. ~ BARREN
A11. The poor get babies and the rich get this. ~ RICHER
A14. Add this lady to a can and you have an oldtime wet spot. ~ ADA
A15. The best port than can be found in France. ~ SEA
A17. Oldtime drying movement. ~ DRIVE
A18. A mighty small matter. ~ ATOM
A19. Repeal may give this to Will Powers. ~ TEST
A21. Consumers. ~ USERS
A23. This is high grade stuff. (abbr.). ~ MT
A24. To open a keg. ~ TAP
A26. This can support a stenog. ~ KNEE
A27. These carry their wet goods in their trunks. ~ ELMS
A30. This will continue to start the flow of liquor. ~ SPIGOT
A33. After being a red he turned to this. ~ TAN
A34. From now on this is going to be straight. ~ NEAT
A35. This is what made the country dry. ~ WAR
A36. Sesame. ~ TIL
A38. This may start delirium tremens. ~ DT
A39. He wants to see a little change. ~ REBEL
A41. There is not much time left for the liquor to do this before Christmas. ~ RIPEN
A43. She makes the cook repeal the potatoes. ~ MA
A45. We hope repeal or Nira will bring this back. ~ WAD
A46. What the milk business has to say about repeal. ~ MOO
A47. A white collar. ~ FOAM
A49. To make a faux pas. ~ ERR
A51. What John Barleycorn will come into. ~ RENOWN
A53. The C. C. C. are saving this. ~ TREE
A54. A survivor of a previous wet era. ~ NOAH
A56. An old fashioned handle. ~ SIR
A57. It almost spells ruin for the speakeasies. ~ RN
A58. This is going to be a sore bunch. ~ TUMOR
A60. Just one glass. ~ PANE
A63. By next spring you will be ready to throw this in the lake. ~ BAIT
A65. Telegram; Friend Wife: Will be gone a week on business. George. ~ SPREE
A67. It is off. ~ LID
A68. The new period. ~ ERA
A69. Dry literature. ~ ESSAYS
A71. The finish of a noble experiment. ~ REPEAL
A73. Ceiling dancers. ~ ELEPHANTS

D1. What they had to do to clamp down the lid. ~ CRAM
D2. A conjunction. ~ OR
D3. The pause that refreshes. ~ REST
D4. Part of the first lap. ~ KNEE
D5. Short term credit. ~ CR
D6. We are this of the lid. ~ RID
D7. A shade often used for a curtain. ~ ECRU
D8. Whiskey without end. ~ WHISK
D9. Repeal will end this for both wets and drys. ~ BATTLE
D10. Unnecessary fuss. ~ ADO
D12. Blessed for Winchell. ~ EVENT
D13. What spinsters do to old love letters. ~ REREAD
D16. When is a still not still? ~ ASTIR
D18. What does the number of this definition recall? ~ AMENDMENT
D20. This will describe the contents. ~ TAG
D22. A wave of this brought about repeal. ~ SENTIMENT
D25. A big shot. ~ POTION
D28. De Face. ~ MAR
D29. This was made from meat and vegetables but now it will be made from liquor. ~ STEW
D30. They tank and tank and tank. ~ SWEDES
D31. "Thish is my besh frien'." ~ PAL
D32. A little lean. ~ TIP
D37. This is not right. ~ LEFT
D40. Where all the mugs are lined up. ~ BAR
D41. Just one bottle after another. ~ ROW
D42. The answer is neither this nor that. ~ NOR
D44. Wake up! ~ AROUSE
D46. The point of the story. ~ MORAL
D48. A pick-up. ~ AERIAL
D50. These are, inclined to make beer go up. ~ RAMPS
D52. A little shot. ~ NIP
D55. This one voted neigh, to save his neck. ~ HORSE
D59. We know on good authority that this is true. ~ REAL
D61. The eagle brought this baby. ~ NIRA
D62. Adam's ale was first served in this garden. ~ EDEN
D63. Chasers with a sting in them. ~ BEES
D64. A bunch of stars in one picture. ~ ARA
D66. Cyclop's headlight. ~ EYE
D70. Oil will make it spoil. ~ SP
D72. This rides easy on the hip (abbr.). ~ PT


